<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Adaptive launcher icon. NOT CURRENTLY IN USE.

    AndroidManifest.xml deliberately points android:icon at the flat raster @mipmap/logo, because
    that is the icon Mhirex has always shipped and the launcher icon was reverted to it on request.
    This file therefore does not resolve, and neither does the <monochrome> layer below, which means
    Android 13+ themed icons do NOT work in the current build, despite a v1.0-beta8 changelog line
    claiming they do.

    Everything needed to switch is already prepared here, so enabling it is a one-line manifest change
    (android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"). The layers were built to be correct when that happens:

      - Background was #FFFFFF, which would make the white brand mark invisible. It is now a dark
        violet-to-black radial, echoing the in-app About halo but far darker.
      - The foreground is the white brand mark pre-scaled to the 72/108 safe zone, because the
        launcher masks away roughly the outer third of the canvas.
      - The monochrome layer reuses that same padded asset. Android tints it with a single colour and
        uses only its alpha, so a white-on-transparent silhouette is exactly the right shape for it.

    To verify the change actually took effect, do not trust the build log. Run:
      aapt2 dump badging <apk> | grep application-icon
    Every density line must point at res/mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml. If it still points at a
    .png, the manifest was not changed.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_bg_mhirex"/>
    <foreground android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_mark"/>
    <monochrome android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_mark"/>
</adaptive-icon>
